WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.230
Drone operations depend on command, telemetry, video, and navigation signals.

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:15.462
Urban radio congestion, metal structures, power infrastructure, terrain, foliage, and the aircraft's orientation can reduce link quality or create intermittent behavior.

00:00:15.462 --> 00:00:22.056
Satellite-navigation performance depends on sky visibility, satellite geometry, receiver condition, and reflected signals.

00:00:22.056 --> 00:00:28.650
Urban canyons and large surfaces can create multipath, where delayed reflections distort position.

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:34.789
Compass sensors can also be affected by nearby metal or electrical fields.

00:00:34.789 --> 00:00:47.296
Check the launch area for interference and avoid calibrating or launching near vehicles, reinforced structures, large metal objects, or energized equipment when guidance warns against it.

00:00:47.296 --> 00:00:52.525
Move to a safer area rather than normalizing unexplained alerts.

00:00:52.525 --> 00:00:56.391
Loss-of-signal planning must be completed before flight.

00:00:56.391 --> 00:01:02.985
Understand the aircraft's configured response, home-point status, route, altitude assumptions, terrain, and obstacles.

00:01:02.985 --> 00:01:10.034
The correct response differs by site and must fit current official and manufacturer guidance.

00:01:10.034 --> 00:01:14.809
Return-to-home is an automated contingency, not a universal rescue.

00:01:14.809 --> 00:01:25.041
An incorrect home point, unsuitable return altitude, changed recovery area, degraded navigation, or obstacle outside sensor capability can create new risk.

00:01:25.041 --> 00:01:29.361
Monitor signal trends and navigation behavior during flight.

00:01:29.361 --> 00:01:34.136
Recover early when quality degrades or position becomes unreliable.

00:01:34.136 --> 00:01:41.640
Do not continue deeper into a weak-link area because control has not yet been lost.
